WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.680
Today we are going to see how DeepMind’s&nbsp;
incredible AI teaches these virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:17.820
characters to move, play football/soccer&nbsp;
and so much more. Now when reading this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:24.000
paper I expected that they would learn the&nbsp;
basics, but what happened afterwards, I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.720
not expect at all. They learned some absolutely&nbsp;
amazing tricks and even more surprising things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.240
and yes, we are going to look at all of&nbsp;
them, but first, the humble beginnings.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:44.160
Now, this is a physics-based game, so to move,&nbsp;
these AI agents need to use their joints and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.940
produce just the right kind of forces and torque&nbsp;
on them to create sophisticated movements and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:59.280
amazing plays. So, let’s see. Oof. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
we are seeing none of that here. Why?

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.580
Well, of course, because they haven’t&nbsp;
had enough time to learn yet. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.460
are these just the humble beginnings, or&nbsp;
is this it? Is this the limit of their AI?

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:20.760
Not quite! But first, they need to learn to move.&nbsp;
How? Well, scientists at DeepMind say by imitating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:29.880
motion capture data. In other words, this tries&nbsp;
to copy how real humans move. This is not bad, at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:36.480
least it can now perform some basic movements, so&nbsp;
that’s great. However, that is not nearly enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:45.000
look. It cannot control the ball yet. Not even&nbsp;
close. But once again, let’s come back 5 days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.560
later and now, can it pull it off? Now, hold&nbsp;
on to your papers, and…oh yes! Yes it can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:02:00.600
and with flying colors! It can now follow a&nbsp;
moving target, dribble, kick, and so much more.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:07.140
Now, get this, we will put these agents&nbsp;
into a training camp for no less than 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:15.120
years and see how they do. We started out&nbsp;
like this, and now. Holy mother of papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:21.960
these little AIs can play! And they can do&nbsp;
so much more than just moving around. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:27.180
they can do these little tricks where the&nbsp;
ball bounces back from an invisible wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:35.040
and not only that, but this player tries to pass&nbsp;
to the other AI this way, and will they score?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:44.880
Oh yes! Fantastic. I have to say, these are&nbsp;
now pretty good players. Good job, little AI!

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:52.380
However, wait a second, what about the 5 years&nbsp;
part? Did scientists at DeepMind have to wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:59.940
for 5 years for this paper and hope that something&nbsp;
good comes out of it? Well, not quite! You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:06.900
in real life, one second passes exactly in&nbsp;
one second. That is not new information to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:13.800
anyone. However, if we have a quick computer,&nbsp;
in a simulation, one second in the game can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:20.400
be simulated in a matter of milliseconds in&nbsp;
real life, thereby speeding up time itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:28.020
Not our time, of course, but the time that the&nbsp;
AI lives in. So this 5-year training camp only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:37.440
took 3 days in real life. That’s not that much.&nbsp;
Hm. Are you thinking what I am thinking? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.180
if it’s so quick, let’s run it&nbsp;
for even longer. See what happens.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:49.560
They ran it for 50 days, and let’s see the&nbsp;
level of plays these can now do. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.740
I saw these results, I almost fell off the&nbsp;
chair. They have become even better. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.560
see what they have learned. Now, they not&nbsp;
only can think a few steps ahead, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:07.380
are even anticipating the behavior of their&nbsp;
teammates and position themselves accordingly.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.500
They also learned to use body to&nbsp;
body contact to their advantage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:21.540
High kicks are also being performed to catch the&nbsp;
defenders off-guard, and I absolutely loved how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:27.960
they developed these quick turns to get away&nbsp;
with the ball. This is incredible. I love it.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:34.080
And they can even play their way out of&nbsp;
really difficult situations. Look at this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:41.280
Oh boy, the defending red player would have to&nbsp;
clear this ball without scoring an own goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.280
so it would have to kick the ball in this&nbsp;
direction, and the margin of error is razor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.600
thin. So far, good thinking, but can it&nbsp;
pull this off? Wow. Good job, little AI!

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.880
The saves they are capable of are&nbsp;
also outstanding. And as we don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.560
really have a referee around, these&nbsp;
AIs haven’t exactly been encouraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.960
to be gentle with each other,&nbsp;
so this can happen too. Ouch.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:17.280
And here is one of my favorite moments, after&nbsp;
a little altercation, the red player falls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.920
and it almost seems like throwing a&nbsp;
little tantrum. This is super fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.600
especially that he was the one who&nbsp;
started it. I am sure some of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.700
Fellow Scholars will point out that this&nbsp;
is not unlike some real football players.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:41.940
And of course, this little AI appears so injured&nbsp;
it cannot get up, but later when it is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:48.180
needed to be there to defend, look! A miracle&nbsp;
happened! And of course, once again, this guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:54.780
just cannot help it. This is one of the most&nbsp;
fun papers I’ve read in a while. So cool.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.140
However, we are experienced&nbsp;
Fellow Scholars over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:03.000
so we would like to know so much&nbsp;
more than what we’ve seen here. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.100
let’s look under the hood and see some&nbsp;
more about what they have really learned.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.140
These are the curves that I am looking&nbsp;
for, yes, and I see that their Elo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:20.040
ratings increase over time. This means that&nbsp;
as they learn more, they get better. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:26.400
very reassuring, however, I still haven’t seen&nbsp;
what my heart desires yet, so let’s continue.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:34.500
Now we’re talking! More detailed data. This is&nbsp;
so cool to see, look. Over time, they can get up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:42.720
quicker, on average, they use their joints better&nbsp;
to run faster, and yes! There we go. Division of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:51.180
labor. Yes yes yes! Wow. That is what my heart&nbsp;
desires. This is where we struck gold. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:57.540
the graph that shows that these two players&nbsp;
learned not only to move properly, but how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:04.860
work together as a team. This is also reflected in&nbsp;
their passing frequency, which is increasing over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:12.240
time. But the division of labor, that is where the&nbsp;
real magic happens. You see, this is one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:19.140
elusive qualities that is quite difficult to learn&nbsp;
properly, often even for humans, and it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:26.940
shows here too. How? Well, look, this is one of&nbsp;
the very few cases in the paper that does not show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:34.740
steady linear growth. At times, it even worsens&nbsp;
over time. After one day of training, the agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:41.700
know just enough to score a goal alone, and thus&nbsp;
they become quite selfish. We all know that guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:51.120
don’t we? But, over time, they realize that as a&nbsp;
team, they can perform even better. Wow. What an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.600
incredible piece of work. And this is the moment&nbsp;
where I got goosebumps when reading this paper. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:04.560
love moments like this and I am so happy to share&nbsp;
it with you. Remember, this is where we started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.740
and this is where we ended up, and all this&nbsp;
through the power of modern learning algorithms.

